## Firewall 2 — Enterprise / Industrial DMZ

This pfSense firewall separates the Level 4 Enterprise network from the Industrial DMZ (Level 3.5).

### Interfaces

| Interface | IP Address | Network |
|---|---|---|
| WAN | `10.10.4.254/24` | Enterprise Network |
| LAN | `10.10.35.1/24` | Industrial DMZ |

### NAT

Outbound NAT is disabled on this firewall.

### WAN Firewall Rules

The firewall restricts access from the Enterprise network into the Industrial DMZ to the IT Desktop used in the exercise.

| Action | Source | Destination | Protocol / Port | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Pass | `10.10.4.102` | `10.10.35.120` | ICMP | Connectivity testing to the Jump Server |
| Pass | `10.10.4.102` | `10.10.35.120` | TCP/3389 | RDP access to the Jump Server |
| Pass | `10.10.4.102` | This firewall | ICMP | Firewall connectivity testing |
| Pass | `10.10.4.102` | This firewall | TCP/80 | pfSense WebConfigurator access |
| Block | Any | Any | Any | Block all other traffic towards the IDMZ |

### LAN Behaviour

The Industrial DMZ uses `10.10.35.1` as its gateway.

The Jump Server is configured as:

- IP address: `10.10.35.120/24`
- Default gateway: `10.10.35.1`

The current configuration retains the default IPv4 `LAN net → any` allow rule on the IDMZ-facing interface.

### Notes

This firewall intentionally exposes only a narrow path from the Enterprise network into the Industrial DMZ.

The IT Desktop at `10.10.4.102` is the only authorised Enterprise host allowed to reach the Jump Server, and RDP is the primary access method.
All other inbound traffic from the Enterprise side is explicitly blocked.
